0:14:30.889514576Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"HasSum.tendsto_sum_nat\\|tendsto_sum_nat\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Topology"]},{"seq":99,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:14:35.329222412Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"tendsto_sum_nat\\|HasSum.tendsto\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Topology/Algebra"]},{"seq":100,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:14:40.810338275Z","text":"`HasSum.tendsto_sum_nat` exists but it's the FORWARD direction (HasSum → Tendsto). I need the REVERSE: Tendsto → HasSum. In a complete space, if the partial sums converge, then `HasSum` holds. Let me find this:"},{"seq":101,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:14:40.813477199Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"hasSum_iff_tendsto\\|tendsto.*HasSum\\|Summable.*hasSum\\|hasSum_of_cauchy\\|cauchy."]},{"seq":102,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:14:45.114091618Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"def HasSum\\|theorem HasSum\\|protected.*HasSum\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/To"]},{"seq":103,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:14:49.822202919Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Topology/Algebra/InfiniteSum/Defs.lean"]},{"seq":104,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:14:57.570023234Z","text":"Interesting! The comment explicitly says `(-1)^n/(n+1)` does NOT have a `HasSum` with the unconditional filter! This is because the series is conditionally convergent, not absolutely convergent. `HasSum` with the default `unconditional` filter requires absolute convergence.\n\nSo `HasSum (fun n => (-1)^n/(n+1)) (log 2)` is FALSE for Mathlib's default `HasSum`! The correct formulation would use the *conditional* summation filter or `HasSum` along the natural numbers order.\n\nThis changes everything. The problem statement says \"prove Σ(-1)ⁿ/(n+1) = log 2\", which in Lean should be formulated as the limit of partial sums, not `HasSum` (which requires unconditional convergence).\n\nLet me re-read the problem: \"Apply the dominated convergence theorem to give a rigorous justification of the fact that Σ_{n=0}^∞ (-1)ⁿ/(n+1) = log 2.\"\n\nThe \"fact\" is that the series sums to log 2. In Lean, the correct formulation is `Tendsto (fun n => ∑ i ∈ range n, (-1)^i/(i+1)) atTop (𝓝 (log 2))`. The `tsum` version requires summability with the unconditional filter, which doesn't hold here. But there IS a `tsum` that works with the conditional filter.\n\nLet me check `Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.InfiniteSum.ConditionalInt`:"},{"seq":105,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:14:57.573644969Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"HasSum.*conditional\\|ConditionalSummable\\|summable_conditional\\|tsum_conditional"]},{"seq":106,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:15:02.154564551Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Topology/Algebra/InfiniteSum/ConditionalInt.lean"]},{"seq":107,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:15:08.320791848Z","text":"Not quite what I need. The key fact: the informal solution uses the `HasSum` formulation, but Mathlib's `HasSum` (unconditional) doesn't work for conditionally convergent series. I should formulate the result as `Tendsto` of partial sums instead. Let me rewrite the theorem:"},{"seq":108,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:15:08.321207420Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":109,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:15:13.142520855Z","text":"Also remove the tsum version since it's not well-defined:"},{"seq":110,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:15:13.142933901Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":111,"type":"t