text","at":"2026-07-26T20:20:32.109077947Z","text":"I need to close the `hf_zero` proof — it needs `congr` to convert `(↑i+1)⁻¹` to `1/(↑i+1)`:"},{"seq":179,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:20:32.118503046Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":180,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:20:37.141781640Z","text":"Now fix the `hx` reference issue. Looking at the error, `hx0` and `hx1` are defined but the code after references `hx` which no longer exists. Let me also look at the remaining structure to see the `hx_abs` usage:"},{"seq":181,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:20:37.142409524Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":182,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:20:41.438483478Z","text":"The `hx_abs` uses `hx` but `hx` was destructured into `hx0, hx1`. Let me fix:"},{"seq":183,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:20:41.439263043Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":184,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:20:55.219895374Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -25"]},{"seq":185,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:20:59.474670515Z","text":"The `one_div_le_one_div` has a different signature than I expected. Let me check:"},{"seq":186,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:20:59.478737821Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -B1 -A2 \"theorem one_div_le_one_div\\b\\|lemma one_div_le_one_div\\b\" .lake/packages/mat"]},{"seq":187,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:08.736723049Z","text":"`one_div_le_one_div (ha : 0 < a) (hb : 0 < b) : 1/a ≤ 1/b ↔ b ≤ a`. So I need TWO positivity proofs and then `b ≤ a`. My arguments are backwards! I need `1/(↑i+1) ≤ 1/(↑j+1) ↔ ↑j+1 ≤ ↑i+1`. So `a = ↑i+1`, `b = ↑j+1`, and the condition `b ≤ a` is `↑j+1 ≤ ↑i+1` which is FALSE (since `i ≤ j`).\n\nI have the direction wrong. `1/a ≤ 1/b ↔ b ≤ a` when `a, b > 0`. Since `i ≤ j`, `↑i+1 ≤ ↑j+1`, so `1/(↑j+1) ≤ 1/(↑i+1)`, which gives `↑i+1 ≤ ↑j+1` ↔ `1/(↑j+1) ≤ 1/(↑i+1)`... Wait, that means my antitone proof should be:\n\n`1/(↑j+1) ≤ 1/(↑i+1)` (which is `1/b ≤ 1/a` with `a=↑i+1, b=↑j+1`), using `one_div_le_one_div h_ai h_aj |>.mpr` with the condition `↑i+1 ≤ ↑j+1` which is `hle`.\n\nSo: `one_div_le_one_div (0 < ↑i+1) (0 < ↑j+1) |>.mpr hle : 1/(↑j+1) ≤ 1/(↑i+1)`. But Antitone requires `f j ≤ f i` when `i ≤ j`, which is `1/(↑j+1) ≤ 1/(↑i+1)`."},{"seq":188,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:08.737129427Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":189,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:19.856401018Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":190,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:26.675650947Z","text":"`one_div_le_one_div ha hb : 1/a ≤ 1/b ↔ b ≤ a`. So `.mpr` expects `b ≤ a`, but I have `a ≤ b` (i.e., `↑i+1 ≤ ↑j+1`). Since I want `1/(↑j+1) ≤ 1/(↑i+1)`, which is `1/b ≤ 1/a` with `a=↑i+1, b=↑j+1`. So I need `one_div_le_one_div h_bj h_ai` (swap the order of arguments!):"},{"seq":191,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:26.676082891Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":192,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:31.685900650Z","text":"Also fix the `hf_zero` — `this.add` needs the right order:"},{"seq":193,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:31.686413847Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":194,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:43.391898777Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -25"]},{"seq":195,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:50.441719678Z","text":"Progress! Three issues:\n1. `tendsto_natCast_atTop_atTop.add` — wrong order (`.add` takes `Tendsto f` and `Tendsto g` gives `Tendsto (f+g)`). Need `tendsto_const_nhds.add` or explicit.\n2. `hx_abs` — `abs_lt` gives `-1 < x ∧ x < 1` but I have `0 < x ∧ x < 1`, so `-1 < x` from `0 < x`.\n3. `hasSum_pow_div_log_of_abs_lt_one` gives `x^(n+1)` not `(-x)^(n+1)`. I need to substitute `x ↦ -x`.\n\nLet me fix all three:"},{"seq":196,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:50.442425268Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":197,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:56.112628145Z","text":"Now fix `hx_abs` and the `hasSum_pow_div_log` substitution. For `hx_abs`: since `0 < x`, `-x < 0 < 1`, so `-1 < -x`... wait, `|x| < 1` means `-1 < x ∧ x < 1`. From `hx0 : 0 < x` and `hx1 : x < 1`: `-1 < 0 < x < 1`, so `-1 < x` and `x < 1`. So `hx_abs = ⟨(by linarith), hx1⟩`."},{"seq":198,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:56.122161770Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":199,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:21:56.246377848Z","text":"[auto-continue 5/8]"},{"seq":200,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:22:04.169769494Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -30"]},{"seq":201,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:22:09.733746690Z","text":"Three issues. Let me fix: (1) `neg_linarith` doesn't exist, use `linarith`, (2) `tendsto_const_nhds` param name, (3) the `hasSum_pow_div_log_of_abs_lt_one` with `-x`."},{"seq":202,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:22:09.734240060Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":203,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:22:15.304306457Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":204,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:22:26.831736596Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":205,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:22:32.840132545Z","text":"Good progress! The `tendsto_const_nhds.add` type issue: `tendsto_const_nhds` gives `Tendsto (fun _ => c) l (𝓝 c)` where `c` needs to be inferred as `(1 : ℝ)`. The `.add` on `Tendsto` expects matching filter types. Let me use explicit formulation:"},{"seq":206,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:22:32.840587051Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":207,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:22:39.245804583Z","text":"Now fix the `1 - -x` vs `1 + x` issue. `hasSum_pow_div_log_of_abs_lt_one` with `x` gives `HasSum (fun n => x^(n+1)/(n+1)) (-log(1-x))`. With `x = -x` (the variable), we get `HasSum (fun n => (-x)^(n+1)/(n+1)) (-log(1-(-x)))` = `(-log(1+x))`. So `-log(1 - (-x)) = -log(1+x)`. This is just `1 - (-x) = 1 + x`. Let me fix the `h_ps`:"},{"seq":208,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:22:39.255486585Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":209,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:22:50.797907063Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":210,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:22:59.119973281Z","text":"The `tendsto_const_nhds` has type `Tendsto (fun _ => ?c) ?l (𝓝 ?c)` but `.add` expects matching domains. The issue is that `tendsto_const_nhds` has `l` and `𝓝 ?c` as metavariables while `tendsto_natCast_atTop_atTop : Tendsto Nat.cast atTop atTop`. They need to unify on `atTop` and `atTop`/`𝓝 ...`. Let me be more explicit:"},{"seq":211,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:22:59.120847706Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":212,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:23:13.982290169Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":213,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:23:25.068451469Z","text":"The `.add` on `Tendsto` expects both to have the same codomain filter type. `h1 : Tendsto ... atTop (𝓝 1)` and `h2 : Tendsto ... atTop atTop`. These don't match — `𝓝 1 ≠ atTop`. I need `h2` to have codomain `𝓝 (∞)` or rather `atTop` IS the right codomain for `tendsto_inv_atTop_zero.comp`. \n\nThe issue is structural: `tendsto_natCast_atTop_atTop : Tendsto Nat.cast atTop atTop` means `↑i → atTop` in the order topology, and `tendsto_inv_atTop_zero` is `Tendsto (fun x => x⁻¹) atTop (𝓝 0)`. So I need `(↑i + 1)` to be `Tendsto atTop atTop`, not `Tendsto atTop (𝓝 1)`.\n\n`Tendsto.add` gives `Tendsto (fun x => f x + g x) l (𝓝 (a + b))` when `Tendsto f l (𝓝 a)` and `Tendsto g l (𝓝 b)`. This gives `𝓝 (1 + ∞)` which doesn't make sense.\n\nI need `atTop_add_atTop` or similar. Let me use a direct proof:"},{"seq":214,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:23:25.068882932Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":215,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:23:38.458398921Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -25"]},{"seq":216,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:23:43.935582695Z","text":"The `rw` fails because the pattern `1 / (?i + 1)` uses a different coercion. Let me use `Tendsto.congr` instead:"},{"seq":217,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:23:43.936236620Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":218,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:23:57.132523353Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":219,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:24:04.156767101Z","text":"Almost! The `congr` needs `((↑i)⁻¹ : ℝ) = ((↑(i+1))⁻¹ : ℝ)` which follows from `↑i = ↑(i+1)`... wait that's FALSE. `↑i ≠ ↑(i+1)`. The issue: `tendsto_inv_atTop_zero.comp tendsto_natCast_atTop_atTop` has type `Tendsto (fun i => (↑i)⁻¹) atTop (𝓝 0)` and I need `Tendsto (fun i => 1/(↑i+1)) atTop (𝓝 0)`. The functions are different: `(↑i)⁻¹ ≠ (↑i+1)⁻¹`.\n\nI need `1/(↑(i+1))` not `1/(↑i)`. The base tendsto should be for `↑(i+1)` not `↑i`. Let me use a proper shifting tendsto:"},{"seq":220,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:24:04.157514877Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":221,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:24:11.920262789Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":222,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:24:18.801727099Z","text":"Syntax error with `apply Tendsto atTop atTop at`. Let me use a simpler approach:"},{"seq":223,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:24:18.802365069Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":224,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:24:31.817616597Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":225,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:24:39.487584643Z","text":"The `tendsto_atTop_mono` + `convert` is a mess. Let me just use a completely direct approach — the `tendsto_atTop_iff` characterization:"},{"seq":226,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:24:39.488544861Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":227,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:24:52.644714511Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":228,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:24:59.436310184Z","text":"Time is running short. Let me simplify the `hf_zero` proof and then commit whatever compiles. Let me use a more basic approach:"},{"seq":229,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:24:59.437121983Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":230,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:25:13.995768121Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":231,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:25:26.301328810Z","text":"The `convert` approach is also problematic. Time to commit a partial. Let me simplify the whole proof and commit with a sorry:"},{"seq":232,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:25:26.301726309Z","tools":["write_file"],"previews":["write_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":233,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:25:37.957411389Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -10"]},{"seq":234,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:25:44.332104196Z","text":"Good — 2 sorries but compiles. Let me update status and commit:"},{"seq":235,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:25:44.332659624Z","tools":["write_file"],"previews":["write_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/status.json"]},{"seq":236,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:25:44.561895607Z","text":"[auto-continue 6/8]"},{"seq":237,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:25:51.840237073Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && git add problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean problems/a93J02/status.json && git commit -m \"a93J0"]},{"seq":238,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:25:59.693822609Z","text":"Now let me try to close the 2 sorries. First, `hf_zero` — prove `1/(↑i+1) → 0`:"},{"seq":239,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:25:59.694438325Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":240,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:26:07.709255198Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":241,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:26:17.365963394Z","text":"The `tendsto_atTop_mono` + `filter_upwards` is conflicting. The `Nat.cast_le.mpr` approach is cleaner but `filter_upwards` can't be used inside `tendsto_atTop_mono`'s second argument which expects a `Tendsto`. Let me simplify:"},{"seq":242,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:26:17.366871635Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":243,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:26:26.015639700Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":244,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:26:35.881736470Z","text":"The `tendsto_atTop_mono` expects `f ≤ g` as a function `∀ i, f i ≤ g i`, but `Nat.cast_nonneg _` gives `0 ≤ ↑i`, not `↑(i+1) ≤ ↑i`. I need `↑(i+1) ≥ ↑i` which is `↑i ≤ ↑(i+1)`. But `tendsto_atTop_mono` says: if `∀ i, f i ≤ g i` and `Tendsto f atTop atTop`, then `Tendsto g atTop atTop`. So I need `f ≤ g` where `g = fun i => ↑(i+1)`. If `f = fun i => ↑i`, then `↑i ≤ ↑(i+1)` is `Nat.cast_le.mpr (Nat.le_succ i)`.\n\nThe issue: `tendsto_atTop_mono (fun i => Nat.cast_nonneg _)` gives `fun i => 0 ≤ ↑i` which has the wrong type. The first argument should be `∀ i, f i ≤ g i` for some specific `f`. Let me fix:"},{"seq":245,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:26:35.882688316Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":246,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:26:44.727398076Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":247,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:26:52.226252527Z","text":"Same error. The issue is that `tendsto_atTop_mono` signature might be different. Let me check:"},{"seq":248,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:26:52.232423091Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -B1 -A5 \"theorem tendsto_atTop_mono\\b\\|lemma tendsto_atTop_mono\\b\" .lake/packages/mat"]},{"seq":249,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:26:57.655391979Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"tendsto_atTop_mono\\b\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/ 2>/dev/null | head -5"]},{"seq":250,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:27:02.456287361Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"theorem tendsto_atTop_mono\\b\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/ 2>/dev/null | head"]},{"seq":251,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:27:07.800559424Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Order/Filter/AtTopBot/Tendsto.lean"]},{"seq":252,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:27:20.293908672Z","text":"So `tendsto_atTop_mono (h : ∀ n, f n ≤ g n) : Tendsto f l atTop → Tendsto g l atTop`. It takes `h` then the Tendsto. So my usage is wrong — I have `tendsto_atTop_mono (fun i => ...) tendsto_natCast_atTop_atTop`. But `tendsto_natCast_atTop_atTop : Tendsto Nat.cast atTop atTop` which is `Tendsto f l atTop` where `f = Nat.cast = (↑·)`. So `g` should be `fun i => ↑(i+1)`. And `h : ∀ i, (↑i) ≤ (↑(i+1))`.\n\nBut the error says \"expected type ℝ\" for the function. This means `f` and `g` are being inferred as `ℝ`-valued but the function arg `(fun i => Nat.cast_le.mpr (Nat.le_succ i))` has `Nat.cast_le` returning `(↑i : ?) ≤ (↑j : ?)`. The coercion might not be to `ℝ`. Let me be explicit:"},{"seq":253,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:27:20.294443782Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":254,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:27:28.373427812Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":255,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:27:34.287565551Z","text":"Almost! The `congr` needs `(↑(i+1))⁻¹ = (↑i + 1)⁻¹`. This follows from `↑(i+1) = ↑i + 1` via `Nat.cast_add_one`. But `rw [Nat.cast_add_one]` needs to rewrite `↑(i+1)` in the LHS. The LHS is `(↑(i+1))⁻¹` but it's displayed as `((fun r => r⁻¹) ∘ fun i => ↑(i+1)) i` which reduces to `(↑(i+1))⁻¹`. Let me try `simp` instead:"},{"seq":256,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:27:34.288217716Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":257,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:27:42.595929172Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -10"]},{"seq":258,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:27:47.270743462Z","text":"`hf_zero` is now proven! Only 1 sorry remains (the Abel's theorem application). Let me commit this progress and then attempt the final sorry:"},{"seq":259,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:27:47.294076419Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && git add problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean && git commit -m \"a93J02: hf_zero proven, down to 1"]},{"seq":260,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:27:52.830342011Z","text":"Now let me tackle the final sorry — the Abel's theorem application. The goal is to show `l = log 2`. The proof outline:\n1. Abel gives `Tendsto (fun x => ∑' n, f n * x^n) (𝓝[<] 1) (𝓝 l)`\n2. On `(0,1)`, the tsum equals `log(1+x)/x` (from `hasSum_pow_div_log_of_abs_lt_one`)\n3. `log(1+x)/x → log(2)/1 = log 2` as `x → 1⁻`\n4. By uniqueness: `l = log 2`\n\nLet me write this carefully. The key step is (2) — the power series identity. Let me read the current sorry location:"},{"seq":261,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:27:52.831173099Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":262,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:28:05.940867424Z","text":"Now let me write the Abel application. The tricky part is the power series identity. Let me try a simpler approach — use `tendsto_nhds_unique` with the abel tendsto and the explicit `log(1+x)/x` limit:"},{"seq":263,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:28:05.942001695Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":264,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:28:19.520925206Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -30"]},{"seq":265,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:28:28.324234512Z","text":"Several issues in the algebraic manipulation. The `neg`/`convert` approach is too fragile. Let me simplify the power series derivation drastically — use `HasSum.mul_left` directly instead of going through `.neg`:"},{"seq":266,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:28:28.324827508Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":267,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:28:40.973419302Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":268,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:28:45.922229084Z","text":"The `neg_mul` rewrite fails because the negation is around the whole fraction. Let me use `ring_nf` instead of the explicit rewrite:"},{"seq":269,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:28:45.922880539Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":270,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:28:58.379649651Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":271,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:29:08.185233238Z","text":"The `.neg` gives `-(-(...))` double negation. And `h_ps'` type is wrong. The `convert` approach is creating messy types. Let me use a completely different strategy — directly prove the identity using `ring_nf` and avoid the intermediate `HasSum` manipulations:"},{"seq":272,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:29:08.185902820Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":273,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:29:08.185969843Z","text":"[edit_file ✗ old-string not found in /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean]"},{"seq":274,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:29:08.394411234Z","text":"[auto-continue 7/8]"},{"seq":275,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:29:12.412083531Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":276,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:29:19.977323360Z","text":"The `h_neg.neg` creates double negation. Let me replace the whole `h_ps2` block with a cleaner approach using `ring_nf`:"},{"seq":277,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:29:19.978239831Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":278,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:29:35.785209238Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93J02/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":279,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-26T20:29:40.070162073Z","text":"The `.div_left` doesn't exist as a method. Let me check the correct name:"},{"seq":280,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-26T20:29:40.076805465Z"